+# Performance report: BlockInfo (block counts panel and hover highlight)
+
+**Script**: `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockInfo.client.luau`
+
+**Workload**: a client MicroProfiler capture with about 6,000 placed blocks, on a desktop and on a
+phone. The "Before" numbers come from that capture. This change was written without access to
+Studio, so there are no "After" numbers yet. Expected values are in the table, and the steps to
+measure them are under "For you to verify".
+
+**Bottleneck**: client script time and garbage collection, both from `Script_BlockInfo`. Every
+`RenderStepped`, the old script did work that grew with the number of blocks in the build:
+
+| Per frame, with N blocks | Cost |
+|---|---|
+| `blocksFolder:GetChildren()` called 3 times (counts, hover search, total) | Three new N-element arrays each frame. At 6,000 blocks that is about 150 KB of garbage per frame, about 9 MB/s at 60 FPS. That is the GC label after the script in most frames. |
+| Counting loop: `block.Name` read for every child | N engine property reads |
+| Hover search: `IsA` plus `block.Position` for every child, to find the block nearest the point under the mouse | 2N engine calls. At about 60 to 130 ns per engine call, this alone is about 1 ms at 6,000 blocks. |
+| Panel text rebuilt with `..` and written to `TextLabel.Text` | New strings every frame, even when nothing changed |
+| `RaycastParams.new()` and a new filter table | Small, but new garbage every frame |
+
+All of this grows linearly with the build, so a 30,000-block build would cost about 5 times what
+it does now.
+
+| Metric (client, about 6,000 blocks) | Before | After (expected, not yet measured) |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Script_BlockInfo` per frame, desktop | about 2.5 ms | well under 0.1 ms: one raycast plus at most 27 table lookups |
+| GC after `Script_BlockInfo` | most frames | none from this script while nothing changes |
+| Phone FPS | about 35 | measure on the phone |
+| Cost as the build grows | linear in block count | flat: does not depend on block count |
+
+**Changes**, highest level first:
+
+1. **Event-driven counts instead of per-frame counting.** Per-type counts and the total are kept
+   in Luau tables, updated from `Blocks.ChildAdded` and `ChildRemoved`. The folder is read once
+   at startup.
+2. **A spatial grid for the hover search.** Each block goes into a grid cell (cell size = hover
+   radius = 4 studs) when it is added. To find the hovered block, the script now checks only the
+   3 x 3 x 3 cells around the raycast hit, not every block in the build. A block whose center is
+   within the hover radius is always in one of those cells, so the result is the same block the
+   old full scan found.
+3. **Panel updates only on change.** The label text and `SelectionBox.Adornee` are written only
+   when a count changes or the hovered block changes. The text is built with `table.concat`.
+4. **Reused `RaycastParams`.** Created once. The character filter is reassigned only when the
+   character changes.
+
+**Behavior**: unchanged for players. The panel shows the same lines, counts, and total, and the
+same block is highlighted. Three edge cases differ in theory:
+
+- When two blocks are exactly the same distance from the hit point, the old script picked
+  whichever came first in `GetChildren()` order. The new one picks whichever comes first in its
+  grid. Players had no way to predict either order.
+- Counts and the grid are updated by `ChildAdded` and `ChildRemoved`. The place uses deferred
+  signals, so a new block can show up in the panel up to one frame later than before.
+- The script assumes a block keeps its `Name` and `Position` after it is placed. This holds today:
+  `BlockPlacement` creates anchored blocks, and nothing renames or moves them. If a future
+  feature moves or retypes a block in place, replace it (remove it and add a new one) or extend
+  the script to listen for those property changes.
+
+**Tried and reverted**: none.
+
+**Left alone**:
+
+- The per-frame raycast. It is one engine query (a few microseconds), and the hover has to follow
+  the mouse and the camera every frame.
+- The panel UI. It already has its own small ScreenGui, and it now redraws only when its text
+  changes.
+- The script stays a standalone LocalScript, as it was. Moving it into the Module Loader layout
+  is a separate decision.
+
+**For you to verify** in Studio and on a device:
+
+1. **Same workload, same capture.** Load or build the same 6,000-block build, start a playtest,
+   and open the MicroProfiler (Ctrl+F6). Pause on a normal frame while you move the mouse over the
+   build. `Script_BlockInfo` should be a small fraction of a millisecond, and the GC label right
+   after it should be gone from most frames. Take a capture from the same position and camera
+   angle as before, so the two compare fairly.
+2. **Scaling.** Repeat at about twice the block count (12,000 or more). `Script_BlockInfo` should
+   stay about the same as at 6,000.
+3. **Memory.** In the Developer Console (F9), Memory, check that `LuaHeap` on the client stays flat
+   while you hover without placing blocks. The grid adds a little memory per block, which should
+   be small next to the blocks themselves.
+4. **Behavior checks by hand.**
+   - Place blocks of each type. Each count and the total go up by one per block.
+   - Hover a block. It gets the yellow outline, and "Pointing at: <type>" appears. Move along a
+     wall of mixed blocks: the outline follows the block under the mouse, as before.
+   - Hover the baseplate right next to a block. The block still highlights, as before, because
+     its center is within 4 studs of the hit point.
+   - Point at the sky. The outline and the "Pointing at" line go away.
+   - Reset your character. Hovering still works, and your own character is never in the way.
+   - Join a server that already has blocks. The counts are correct right away.
+5. **Phone.** Publish, then play the same build on the phone that ran at about 35 FPS. Check the
+   frame rate (Developer Console, or Shift+F5 on desktop for comparison). Phone frame rate needs a
+   published build on a real device, so Studio cannot show it.
